Step 1: The Imports (Gathering the Tools)

    Before we build anything, we need to bring in our toolboxes.

    Python:

        import discord
        import requests
        import os
        from dotenv import load_dotenv

    import discord: This brings in the official Discord library. It contains all the blueprints for
                    connecting to Discord's servers, reading messages, and typing in channels.

    import requests: This is our "Courier." It is the library we use to send data packages
                      over the internet to your Render API.

    import os: This stands for Operating System. It allows our Python script to look inside your
               computer's folders and read hidden files.

    from dotenv import load_dotenv: This is our lockpick. It allows Python to open and read your hidden .env file
                                    where your passwords are kept.

Step 2: Configuration & Security

    Python:

        load_dotenv()
        TOKEN = os.getenv("DISCORD_TOKEN")
        API_URL = "https://your-render-app-name.onrender.com/chat"

    load_dotenv(): This is a function call. It tells Python, "Look in this folder for a file named .env and
                   load all the secrets inside it into memory."

    os.getenv("DISCORD_TOKEN"): This is a method. We are asking the OS to get the environment variable named DISCORD_TOKEN.
                                We save that password into the TOKEN variable.

    API_URL: This is just a string variable. It is the exact GPS coordinate of your Render "Kitchen."

Step 3: Building the Bot's Body & Senses (Classes)

    Discord values privacy. If you just drop a bot into a server, it is blind and deaf by default.
    We have to explicitly give it "Intents" (senses).

    Python:

        intents = discord.Intents.default()
        intents.message_content = True
        client = discord.Client(intents=intents)

    discord.Intents.default(): Intents is a Class (a blueprint). We are asking Discord for the default set of permissions
                               (like seeing when people log in or out).

    intents.message_content = True: This is crucial. We are modifying a property of the Intents class. We are explicitly
                                    turning ON the bot's ability to read the actual text people type.

    discord.Client(intents=intents): Client is a massive Class. Think of Client as the physical, robotic body of your bot.
                                     We are building this robot body and plugging the intents (senses) we just defined into
                                     its head. We name this robot client.

Step 4: Event 1 - Waking Up

    In Discord programming, bots don't run top-to-bottom like a normal script. They sit in a loop waiting for Events to happen.

    Python:

        @client.event
        async def on_ready():
            print(f"✅ {client.user} has connected to Discord and is listening!")

    @client.event: This is called a Decorator. Think of it like a tripwire. We attach this tripwire to our client robot.

    async def: async stands for asynchronous. It means "do this in the background without freezing the rest of the program."

    on_ready(): This is a built-in Discord event. The moment your bot successfully logs into Discord's servers,
                this tripwire snaps, and it triggers the code inside.

    client.user: This grabs the bot's username (e.g., Super Agent Swarm#1234) and prints it to your terminal so you know it's alive.

Step 5: Event 2 - Hearing a Message

    This is the main engine of the bot. Every single time anyone sends any message in the server, this tripwire snaps.

    Python:

        @client.event
        async def on_message(message):

    on_message(message): Whenever a message is sent, Discord grabs all the data (who sent it, what time,
                         what channel, what the text was) and stuffs it into a package called message.

    The Filters (Ignoring the Noise)--

    Because the bot hears everything, we must tell it what to ignore.

        Python:
            if message.author == client.user:
                return

    Usage: If the bot sends a message, it will hear its own message and try to reply to itself in an infinite loop.
           This tells it: "If the author of the message is you (client.user), return (stop reading and go back to sleep)."

        Python:
            if client.user in message.mentions:

    Usage: We check the message.mentions list (a list of everyone tagged in the text). If our bot's name is in that list,
            we proceed. If not, the bot ignores the message and lets human friends talk in peace.

    Cleaning the Data--

        Python:
                prompt = message.content.replace(f'<@{client.user.id}>', '').strip()

    Usage: If you type @Super Agent Swarm Hello!, the actual text Discord sees is <@123456789> Hello!.

    .replace() and .strip(): We use these string methods to cut the ugly tag out of the text and delete any extra spaces,
                             leaving us with a clean prompt variable containing just: "Hello!"

    The Cloud Connection (The Bridge)--

        Python
                async with message.channel.typing():

    Usage: This is a beautiful UI feature. It tells Discord to show the little "Super Agent Swarm is typing..."
           animation in the channel. Because it's an async with block, it keeps animating automatically while the rest of the code runs.

        Python:

            payload = {
                "user_id": str(message.author.id),
                "prompt": prompt
            }

    Usage: This is where we build the Pydantic package for your Render API. We grab the human's unique Discord ID
           (message.author.id), convert it to a string, and pack it with their question.
           This ensures perfect memory separation on the backend.

        Python:
                try:
                    response = requests.post(API_URL, json=payload)

    Usage: The Courier takes flight. We use the requests library to send a POST request to your Render API_URL,
           carrying the payload box. The code pauses right here and waits for Render to finish thinking.

        Python:
                if response.status_code == 200:
                    data = response.json()
                    await message.reply(data["final_answer"])

    Usage: If Render replies with a 200 (Internet code for "Success"), we unpack the JSON box, extract
           the final_answer string, and use await message.reply() to make the bot drop the answer directly into the Discord channel,
           tagging the user who asked.

Step 6: The Ignition

        Python:
            client.run(TOKEN)

    Usage: None of the code above actually runs until this final line. client.run is the ignition switch.
           It takes your secret password (TOKEN), connects the robot body (client) to the internet, and starts the infinite listening loop.

    🚧 Problems Faced During bot.py Setup

    As requested, here are the specific engineering roadblocks we hit while building this file, and how they connect to the code:

    1. The "Blind Bot" Problem (Relates to Step 3)

    What happened: You successfully invited the bot to your server, but when you typed messages, it completely ignored you.

    The Cause: Discord recently updated their security policies to prevent bots from secretly reading user data.
               Even though our code had intents.message_content = True, Discord blocked it on their end.

    The Fix: We had to go into the Discord Developer Portal (the "Back of House") and manually flip the
             "Message Content Intent" switch to grant the bot 20/20 vision.

    2. The "Staging Environment" Disconnect (Relates to Step 2)

    What happened: When you built your second Swarm (Multi-Tenancy), the new bot wouldn't answer you.

    The Cause: You cloned the folder, but bot.py was still pointing to the API_URL of your first Render server,
               and it was missing the new .env token.

    The Fix: You updated the API_URL string on Line 10 to point to the new Kitchen, and ran python
             bot.py to turn the ignition key on the new client.